  facts: ["Congress created the Freedmen's Bureau on March 3, 1865, within the War Department; Oliver Otis Howard was appointed Commissioner in May 1865, and the Bureau was not abolished until 1872, though most of its work took place between June 1865 and December 1868.", "President Andrew Johnson vetoed the Freedmen's Bureau Bill on February 19, 1866, objecting that it would extend military jurisdiction over civilians in peacetime, with agents acting as judges without juries, in eleven states without representation in Congress; the Senate failed to override that veto, and Congress overrode his veto of a second bill in July 1866.", "Generals James Steedman and Joseph Fullerton, appointed by Johnson, inspected the Bureau in 1866 (instructions of April 7; first report May 8, 1866), criticized agents who interfered 'arbitrarily' in planter-laborer relations and officers who ran plantations and sawmills, praised 'judicious' officers, and recommended turning Bureau duties over to the army.", "After the Military Reconstruction Acts of March 1867, Bureau Circular No. 9 (May 1, 1867) said measures would be taken to inform freedmen entitled to be registered and urged them to disregard threats; district army commanders held the legal authority over registration, and sources describe the Bureau as informing and protecting freedmen and, in some places, assisting or supervising registration (the U.S. Capitol Visitor Center describes Congress as charging the Bureau with supervising elections including registration).", "In late 1865 the legislatures of Mississippi and South Carolina, among others, passed Black Codes that required written yearly labor contracts, treated the unemployed as vagrants, and limited freed people's rights.", "General William T. Sherman's Special Field Order No. 15 (January 1865) set aside coastal land in South Carolina and Georgia for freed families; after Johnson's amnesty and pardons most of that land was returned to former owners in 1865-1866.", "The Fisk Free Colored School opened in Nashville in January 1866, founded by American Missionary Association representatives (Edward P. Smith, Erastus M. Cravath) with Bureau education superintendent John Ogden; General Clinton B. Fisk helped secure former Union army hospital barracks for it.", "Howard University was chartered by Congress on March 2, 1867, and named for Commissioner O. O. Howard, a founder; the Bureau helped fund its early campus and built a medical building and hospital there in 1869.", "Historians of the Dunning school, named for William A. Dunning, portrayed Reconstruction as misgovernment; Du Bois challenged that view in Black Reconstruction in America (1935), and most historians abandoned the Dunning interpretation by the 1960s (Eric Foner)."],
